## Solution

**Setup.** Let $f(L)$ denote the expected number of cuts starting from a beam of length $L$, with the process stopping once the retained segment is shorter than $0.5$ m. Thus
$$f(L)=0,\quad L<0.5.$$

For $L\ge 0.5$ one cut is made. The cut point $U\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(0,L)$; the retained segment is $V=\max(U,L-U)$, which is uniform on $[L/2,\,L]$ with density $2/L$. Conditioning on $V$,
$$f(L)=1+\frac{2}{L}\int_{L/2}^{L}f(v)\,dv,\qquad L\ge 0.5. \tag{1}$$

**Normalization.** Set $t=2L$ and $h(t)=f(t/2)$, so the stopping threshold becomes $t=1$:
$$h(t)=0\;(t<1),\qquad h(t)=1+\frac{2}{t}\int_{t/2}^{t}h(s)\,ds\;(t\ge 1). \tag{2}$$
We seek $f(2)=h(4)$.

**Interval $[1,2]$.** Here $t/2\in[1/2,1]$, so $h(s)=0$ on $[t/2,1]$ and (2) reduces to
$$h(t)=1+\frac{2}{t}\int_1^t h(s)\,ds.$$
Let $H(t)=\int_1^t h(s)\,ds$, $H(1)=0$. Then $H'(t)=h(t)=1+\frac{2}{t}H(t)$, i.e.
$$H'-\frac{2}{t}H=1.$$
Integrating factor $t^{-2}$: $(Ht^{-2})' = t^{-2}$, giving $H(t)t^{-2}=1-1/t$, hence
$$H(t)=t(t-1),\qquad h(t)=2t-1,\qquad t\in[1,2].$$
In particular $H(2)=2$ and $h(2)=3$.

**Interval $[2,4]$.** Now $t/2\in[1,2]$, so
$$\int_{t/2}^{t}h(s)\,ds=\underbrace{\int_{t/2}^{2}(2s-1)\,ds}_{H(2)-H(t/2)}+\int_2^t h(s)\,ds = 2-\tfrac{t}{2}\!\left(\tfrac{t}{2}-1\right)+K(t),$$
where $K(t)=\int_2^t h(s)\,ds$, $K(2)=0$. Simplifying,
$$\int_{t/2}^{t}h(s)\,ds = 2-\frac{t^2}{4}+\frac{t}{2}+K(t).$$
Substituting into (2):
$$h(t)=K'(t)=1+\frac{2}{t}\!\left(2-\frac{t^2}{4}+\frac{t}{2}+K(t)\right)=2+\frac{4}{t}-\frac{t}{2}+\frac{2}{t}K(t).$$
This gives the linear ODE
$$K'-\frac{2}{t}K=2+\frac{4}{t}-\frac{t}{2},\qquad K(2)=0.$$
With integrating factor $t^{-2}$:
$$(Kt^{-2})' = 2t^{-2}+4t^{-3}-\tfrac12 t^{-1},$$
$$K(t)t^{-2}=\Big[-2s^{-1}-2s^{-2}-\tfrac12\ln s\Big]_2^t = \frac32-\frac{2}{t}-\frac{2}{t^2}+\frac12\ln\frac{2}{t}.$$
Hence
$$K(t)=\frac32\,t^2-2t-2+\frac{t^2}{2}\ln\frac{2}{t}.$$
(Check: $K(2)=6-4-2+0=0$. ✓)

**Evaluating at $t=4$.** Since $t/2=2$, the integral in (2) is exactly $K(4)$:
$$h(4)=1+\frac{2}{4}K(4)=1+\frac12 K(4).$$
$$K(4)=\frac32(16)-8-2+\frac{16}{2}\ln\frac12 = 24-10-8\ln2 = 14-8\ln2.$$
Therefore
$$h(4)=1+\frac12(14-8\ln2)=1+7-4\ln2 = 8-4\ln2.$$

**Result.** The expected number of cuts is

$$\boxed{8-4\ln 2}\;\approx\;5.2274.$$

